+# Prompt: viz-even
+
+Verbatim prompt from the lead (2026-10-16).
+
+---
+
+You are the data-visualisation engineer `viz-even` for melon-seek (repo /home/user/melon-seek, branch claude/stoic-ride-54ddxp), a zero-build vanilla ES-module app, "the Zillow of job postings". You are in your own git worktree. First run `git fetch origin claude/stoic-ride-54ddxp && git checkout --detach origin/claude/stoic-ride-54ddxp`. Read:
+- README.md, docs/CONTRACT.md, docs/strategy/ROADMAP.md §8 (UI simplicity budget), docs/METHODOLOGY_GUIDE.md;
+- docs/process/PLAYBOOK.md (including "Checkpoints"), docs/process/TEMPLATE.md;
+- docs/process/viz-review-beta.md if present, docs/process/salary-audit-data.md and docs/process/salary-audit-backend.md (once present; otherwise the lead's newest sections in docs/process/lead.md);
+- public/viz/chart.js in full.
+
+**The user's complaint (2026-10-11), verbatim:** "Are you sure the salary range of the big tech are correct?! They don't look correct and kinda all span too evenly."
+
+**What the audits found:**
+- The numbers are the companies' own posted figures, and the companies do post fixed-ratio bands (Google top = 1.45 × bottom on 66% of US ranges; Amazon 1.35×; Microsoft about 1.92×), often the same band for many roles at a level.
+- But the backend engineer also found a presentation cause that is ours: "the clusters view puts midpoints into fixed-width bins ($50K at desktop width) and draws one circle per bin at the bin's centre. Circles are therefore always evenly spaced; only their size carries the distribution. The real, spiky shape is in the thin histogram strip above the axis."
+- The ranges view shows rows of identical bars, because many postings carry the same posted band.
+
+**Your job:** make the chart show the real shape of each board's pay, so a job seeker sees where pay actually concentrates, without adding clutter. Then prove it on the real data.
+
+**Steps:**
+1. **Measure first.** On today's crawl, for each big-tech board and two main-site boards (Anthropic, Anduril), per chart row, compare the true distribution of midpoints (quantiles, modes) with what the clusters view draws: circle positions and sizes, bin width at 1440 and 390. Quantify how much the drawing hides, e.g. the distance between a bin's centre and the median of its postings, and modes merged into one bin. Do the same for the ranges view: how many bars are exact duplicates?
+2. **Design.** Options include:
+   - placing each circle at its postings' median (or mean) within the bin, not the bin centre;
+   - adaptive or narrower bins where data is dense;
+   - a dot strip or beeswarm per row;
+   - in the ranges view, stacking identical posted bands into one bar with a count ("× 247"), instead of repeating it.
+
+   Pick the simplest honest option. Keep the visual language and the controls; no new always-visible control (ROADMAP §8). Write the alternatives you rejected, and why, in your log.
+3. **Build it.** Edit public/viz/chart.js, and public/styles.css only if needed (fix-density's one-line rule stays: any new caption is one short line, with detail behind the existing "Details" from public/viz/note.js). Keep keyboard navigation, the tooltips, selection, aria labels, the Location-filter behaviour (SA-5: under a Location filter the chart plots the picked place's range) and Google's Company grouping (google-grouping) working.
+4. **Tests.**
+   - A NEW test/viz-even.test.js (unit: the circle-placement or stacking maths, on synthetic data that fails on the old code).
+   - A NEW test/e2e/viz-even.e2e.js, registered in scripts/e2e.js the way the others are: on a real board, a circle's x matches its postings' median within N px, and duplicate bands are stacked with the right count.
+   - Both must fail on the old code.
+5. **Screenshots:** before and after, per big-tech board at 1440x900 and 390x844, light and dark, in docs/screenshots/viz-even/. Shrink them with `bash scripts/cycle/shrink-screenshots.sh docs/screenshots/viz-even`.
+
+**Data and tools:**
+- Today's crawl is at /tmp/claude-0/-home-user-melon-seek/b6b764de-866b-5af7-92f3-f582ebca24d9/scratchpad/wt-data2/data/snapshots and .../wt-data2/data/beta/snapshots. Symlink them as data/snapshots and data/beta/snapshots in your worktree; never commit them.
+- Build: `node scripts/build-static.js` (your worktree's dist/ is yours).
+- Serve: `node scripts/cycle/serve.mjs --dist dist --pid <pidfile>`.
+- Playwright: NODE_PATH=/tmp/claude-0/-home-user-melon-seek/b6b764de-866b-5af7-92f3-f582ebca24d9/scratchpad/pw/node_modules, Chromium at /opt/pw-browsers/chromium-1194/chrome-linux/chrome. Never `playwright install`; no `pkill -f` / `pgrep -f`; use pid files.
+
+**Ownership:**
+- You own: public/viz/chart.js (the drawing of clusters and ranges, not the notes text), public/styles.css for chart marks only, your tests, docs/process/viz-even.md, docs/process/prompts/viz-even.md (paste this prompt verbatim) and your screenshots.
+- Do not edit server/**, public/app.js, public/api.js, scripts/build-static.js or scripts/snapshot.js. Write a patch to docs/process/patches/viz-even-<name>.diff if you need a change there.
+
+**Rules:**
+- Never cut or hide postings (the user's rule).
+- Plain words.
+- **Resume safety (the user, 2026-10-16):** commit small and often with explicit paths ("WIP: …" is fine), keep a `## State` section at the top of your log, and send the lead each finished, tested piece by SendMessage to "main". The lead checkpoints your worktree every 15 minutes. Never push.
+
+**Verify:**
+- `MELON_BETA_SNAPSHOT_DIR= npm test` with the data links moved OUT of data/ (CI has no data): all green. S-6, PERF-1, V15 and T21 are load-sensitive; re-run them alone.
+- The full e2e suite with data linked: no new failures.
+
+Report: the measurements before/after, the design chosen and rejected, SHAs, test results and screenshots.
